    "narrative": "Our offensive outlook for May 28 is anchored by **Zach McKinstry**, who yields our highest daily efficiency score at 57.26. While he sits in the 6-hole, the matchup against Rodriguez is a prime target; the elevated 4.95 xERA suggests a vulnerability that **Zach McKinstry** is well-positioned to exploit. His 19% boost from the SP skill metric outweighs the slight penalty from his platoon split, making him the most viable asset on a day where our primary speed threats, **Corbin Carroll** and **Jose Caballero**, are sidelined by team off-days. **Austin Riley** (55.38) remains a steady anchor in the 5-hole against Tolle, buoyed by favorable park factors and the stability of his spot in the batting order.\n\nThe most challenging decision today involved the marginal 44.54 score assigned to **Seiya Suzuki**. While his base projection is solid, the encounter with the elite arm of Skenes\u2014who carries an xERA of 3.03\u2014acts as a significant anchor on his ceiling. We ultimately opted to start **Seiya Suzuki** due to the slight wind advantage providing a minor lift, though he remains a high-risk play. With our depth pieces like **Josh Naylor**, **James Wood**, and **Mickey Moniak** currently inactive, we are relying on our core trio to provide volume. We remain cautious regarding **Seiya Suzuki** specifically; if the early innings suggest Skenes is locating his secondary stuff effectively, the potential for a sub-40 performance is high. We are monitoring **Trevor Story**, **Brice Turang**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Max Muncy**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Carson Williams**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Kyle Karros**, **Alex Freeland**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Cole Young**, and **Henry Bolte** for any late-breaking lineup changes that might force a reshuffling of our bench priority.",
